<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<handout component="day14" xml:id="day-14-lecture">
    <title>Day 14</title>

    <introduction>
        <p>
            Ch 5.1.4-5: More cross-validation
        </p>
    </introduction>

    <objectives>
        <title>Covered in this class</title>

        <ul>
            <li>
                <p>
                    Bias-variance tradeoff
                </p>
            </li>

        </ul>
    </objectives>

    <paragraphs>
        <title>Bias-Variance Tradeoff: Bias</title>
    <p>
        <md>
            E(y_0 - \hat{f}(x_0))^2
    = \operatorname{Var}(\hat{f}(x_0))
    + [\operatorname{Bias}(\hat{f}(x_0))]^2
    + \operatorname{Var}(\epsilon)
        </md>
        </p>

        <sidebyside width="50%">
            <stack>
                <p>
                    <ul>
                        <li>
                            <p>
                                We are discussing bias in the estimation of test
                                error rate.
                            </p>
                        </li>

                        <li>
                            <p>
                                Validation set: overestimates test error because
                                it uses a small subset of the data.
                            </p>
                        </li>

                        <li>
                            <p>
                                <m>k</m>-fold CV: has a medium level of bias
                                because each training set has approximately
                                <m>(k-1)n/k</m> observations.
                            </p>
                        </li>

                        <li>
                            <p>
                                LOOCV: gives an approximately unbiased estimate
                                because it uses almost all the data each time.
                            </p>
                        </li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </stack>

            <stack>
                <p>
                    <ul>
                        <li>
                            <p>
                                Based on bias alone, LOOCV is better than
                                <m>k</m>-fold CV.
                            </p>
                        </li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </stack>
        </sidebyside>
    </paragraphs>

    <paragraphs>
        <title>Bias-Variance Tradeoff: Variance</title>

        <p>
            <md>
                E(y_0 - \hat{f}(x_0))^2
    = \operatorname{Var}(\hat{f}(x_0))
    + [\operatorname{Bias}(\hat{f}(x_0))]^2
    + \operatorname{Var}(\epsilon)
            </md>
        </p>

        <sidebyside width="50%">
            <p>
                <ul>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            We are discussing variance in the estimation of test
                            error rate.
                        </p>
                    </li>

                    <li>
                        <p>
                            LOOCV has higher variance:
                            <ul>
                                <li>
                                    <p>
                                        It averages the outputs of <m>n</m>
                                        fitted models, each trained on almost
                                        identical observations.
                                    </p>
                                </li>

                                <li>
                                    <p>
                                        The fitted models are highly correlated
                                        with each other.
                                    </p>
                                </li>

                                <li>
                                    <p>
                                        This is like a repeated-measures problem
                                        in science; the results may not
                                        generalize well across samples.
                                    </p>
                                </li>
                            </ul>
                        </p>
                    </li>
                </ul>
            </p>

            <p>
                <ul>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            <m>k</m>-fold CV:
                            <ul>
                                <li>
                                    <p>
                                        The <m>k</m> fitted models are somewhat
                                        less correlated with each other.
                                    </p>
                                </li>

                                <li>
                                    <p>
                                        The mean of highly correlated quantities
                                        has higher variance than the mean of
                                        uncorrelated quantities.
                                    </p>
                                </li>

                                <li>
                                    <p>
                                        Empirically, <m>k=5</m> or <m>10</m> has
                                        been shown to be a happy medium.
                                    </p>
                                </li>
                            </ul>
                        </p>
                    </li>
                </ul>
            </p>
        </sidebyside>
    </paragraphs>

    <paragraphs>
        <title>In short: Validation vs Test</title>

        <p>
            <ul>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        We often treat the validation set as if it were the test
                        set, even though it is not.
                    </p>
                </li>

                <li>
                    <p>
                        A true test set is an independent sample.
                    </p>
                </li>

                <li>
                    <p>
                        Cross-validation measures never provide that independent
                        sample.
                    </p>
                </li>
            </ul>
        </p>
    </paragraphs>

    <paragraphs>
        <title>Using K-Fold CV on Polynomial Linear Regression</title>

        <p>
            Polynomial regression
        </p>

        <sidebyside width="45%">

                <p>
                    Replace the linear model
                    <md>
                        y_i = \beta_0 + \beta_1 x_i + \epsilon_i
                    </md>
                    with
                    <md>
                        y_i = \beta_0 + \beta_1 x_i + \beta_2 x_i^2
          + \cdots + \beta_d x_i^d + \epsilon_i
                    </md>
                </p>


                <p>
                    <ul>
                        <li>
                            <p>
                                These models can be fit with linear regression
                                by passing in predictors <m>x_i^\ell</m>.
                            </p>
                        </li>

                        <li>
                            <p>
                                We tend not to go higher than degree 3 or 4
                                because the model can become overly flexible.
                            </p>
                        </li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
        </sidebyside>
    </paragraphs>

    <paragraphs>
        <title>Using Linear Regression for fitting polynomial models</title>

        <sidebyside width="50%">
            <p component="inst-notes">
                <ul>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            Draw a column of data.
                        </p>
                    </li>

                    <li>
                        <p>
                            Draw the version with squares and cubes in the
                            columns.
                        </p>
                    </li>

                    <li>
                        <p>
                            Pass it to linear regression and interpret the
                            coefficients as the ones we want.
                        </p>
                    </li>
                </ul>
            </p>

            <p/>
        </sidebyside>
    </paragraphs>
</handout>